end of file is not an error for availableData

A pipe whose writer has finished answers 0 bytes, which is what happens every time a child
process exits, and macOS returns empty data for it. This raised NSFileHandleOperationException
carrying whatever errno was left over from before, so the message read "No such file or
directory" for a read that never touched a path.

iTerm2's pidinfo service died on it, inside -[pidinfo reallyRunShellScript:shell:completion:]:
it runs a shell script and reads the output to the end, and the last read is always this one.
errno is cleared before the read so a real failure is still reported.

--- a/src/NSFileHandle.m
+++ b/src/NSFileHandle.m
@@ -1281,12 +1281,29 @@
         {
             return nil;
         }
+        /*
+         * END OF FILE IS NOT AN ERROR, and treating it as one raised out of an ordinary read. A pipe
+         * whose writer has finished answers 0 bytes, which is what happens every time a child
+         * process exits, and macOS returns empty data for it. This raised
+         * NSFileHandleOperationException carrying whatever errno was left over from before, which is
+         * why the message read "No such file or directory" for a read that never touched a path.
+         *
+         * iTerm2's pidinfo service died on it: it runs a shell script and reads the output to the
+         * end, and the last read is always this one.
+         */
+        errno = 0;
         NSUInteger length = _NSReadFromFileDescriptor(_fd, buf, PAGE_SIZE);
         if (length == 0)
         {
+            int failure = errno;
+
             free(buf);
-            FAIL();
-            return nil;
+            if (failure != 0)
+            {
+                FAIL();
+                return nil;
+            }
+            return [NSData data];
         }
         return [NSData dataWithBytesNoCopy:buf length:length];
     }
